@lesson 05_operators
@title Function and operator overloading
@summary Overloaded functions, operator+, member and friend operators, prefix and postfix ++, operator<<.
@order 5

@section 5.1 Function overloading
@points
- Several functions may share a name in the same scope if their parameter lists differ in the number and/or types of parameters.
- The compiler picks the version that matches the call.
- Functions that differ ONLY in return type cannot be overloaded.
- A string literal such as "Hello" has type const char*, so the parameter must be const char* (not char*).
@code cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class printData {
public:
    void print(int i)           { cout << "Printing int: " << i << endl; }
    void print(double f)        { cout << "Printing double: " << f << endl; }
    void print(const char* c)   { cout << "Printing string: " << c << endl; }
};

int main() {
    printData pd;
    pd.print(5);              // print(int)
    pd.print(500.263);        // print(double)
    pd.print("Hello C++");    // print(const char*)
    return 0;
}
@output 
Printing int: 5
Printing double: 500.263
Printing string: Hello C++

@section 5.2 Operator overloading: operator+
@points
- Most built-in operators can be redefined for user-defined types.
- An overloaded operator is a function named "operator" followed by the symbol, with a return type and parameters like any other function:
Box operator+(const Box& b) const;
- a + b is translated to a.operator+(b) (member function). The left operand is the object the member is called on (*this); the right operand is the parameter.
- Take the parameter by const reference to avoid a copy; mark the member const when it does not modify *this.
- The result is a NEW object; the operands are unchanged.
@code cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Box {
public:
    Box() : length(0), breadth(0), height(0) { }
    double getVolume() const { return length * breadth * height; }
    void setLength(double len)  { length = len; }
    void setBreadth(double bre) { breadth = bre; }
    void setHeight(double hei)  { height = hei; }

    // overload + : adds two Box objects dimension by dimension
    Box operator+(const Box& b) const {
        Box box;
        box.length  = this->length  + b.length;
        box.breadth = this->breadth + b.breadth;
        box.height  = this->height  + b.height;
        return box;
    }

private:
    double length, breadth, height;
};

int main() {
    Box Box1, Box2, Box3;

    Box1.setLength(6.0);  Box1.setBreadth(7.0);  Box1.setHeight(5.0);
    Box2.setLength(12.0); Box2.setBreadth(13.0); Box2.setHeight(10.0);

    cout << "Volume of Box1 : " << Box1.getVolume() << endl;
    cout << "Volume of Box2 : " << Box2.getVolume() << endl;

    Box3 = Box1 + Box2;          // same as Box1.operator+(Box2)
    cout << "Volume of Box3 : " << Box3.getVolume() << endl;
    return 0;
}
@output 
Volume of Box1 : 210
Volume of Box2 : 1560
Volume of Box3 : 5400
@text
Check: volume of Box1 = 6*7*5 = 210; Box2 = 12*13*10 = 1560; Box3 has dimensions (18, 20, 15), volume 5400.

@section 5.3 Member vs non-member operators
@points
- A binary operator can be a member function (one parameter, the right operand) or a free function (two parameters, one per operand):
@code text
  Box operator+(const Box& b) const;              // member
  Box operator+(const Box& a, const Box& b);      // non-member
@points
- Operators like << for streams (cout << obj) cannot be members of your class, because the left operand is the stream. Write them as free functions, declared friend if they need access to private fields.

@section 5.4 Overloading ++ (prefix and postfix) and << for a Clock class
@text
Goal: use Clock objects like this
@code cpp
Clock c1, c2(3,20), c3(10,59);
cout << c1 << " " << c2 << " " << c3 << endl;   // 12:00 03:20 10:59
c1++;  ++c2;  c3++;
@points
- Prefix ++c: the object increments itself, then the result is used. Member form:   Clock& operator++();   body: tick(); return *this; Returning a reference to *this is the usual convention.
- Postfix c++: the OLD value is the result of the expression. Member form:   Clock operator++(int); The int parameter is unused and unnamed; it only tells the compiler "this is the postfix version" (the call is c.operator++(0)). Body: save a copy of the old state, tick(), return the saved copy.
- Postfix is therefore slower (makes a copy); prefer ++c when the value is not needed.
- operator<< is a friend free function that writes to an ostream and returns that ostream so calls can be chained.
- Decrement (--) is overloaded the same way.
- tick() advances one minute: min = (min + 1) % 60, and when min wraps to 0 the hour becomes (hour + 1) % 24.
@code cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Clock {
    friend ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const Clock& c);
public:
    Clock(int h = 12, int m = 0) : hour(h), min(m) { }   // noon by default
    Clock& operator++();        // prefix  ++c : advance, then return the object itself
    Clock operator++(int);      // postfix c++ : the unused int tells the compiler this is postfix
private:
    void tick();                // advance the clock by 1 minute
    int hour, min;
};

void Clock::tick() {
    min = (min + 1) % 60;
    if (min == 0)
        hour = (hour + 1) % 24;
}

ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const Clock& c) {
    if (c.hour < 10) os << '0';       // pad hour with a 0
    os << c.hour << ":";
    if (c.min < 10) os << '0';        // pad minutes with a 0
    os << c.min;
    return os;
}

Clock& Clock::operator++() {          // prefix
    tick();
    return *this;
}

Clock Clock::operator++(int) {        // postfix
    Clock old = *this;                // save the old value
    tick();
    return old;                       // return the value from BEFORE the increment
}

int main() {
    Clock c1, c2(3, 20), c3(10, 59);
    cout << c1 << " " << c2 << " " << c3 << endl;

    c1++;
    ++c2;
    c3++;
    cout << c1 << " " << c2 << " " << c3 << endl;

    c1 = ++c2;                        // c2 is incremented first, then copied to c1
    cout << c1 << " " << c2 << endl;

    c1 = c3++;                        // c1 gets the OLD c3, then c3 is incremented
    cout << c1 << " " << c3 << endl;
    return 0;
}
@output 
12:00 03:20 10:59
12:01 03:21 11:00
03:22 03:22
11:00 11:01
@text
Explanation of the last two lines:
@points
- c1 = ++c2;   c2 goes from 03:21 to 03:22, and c1 receives 03:22.
- c1 = c3++;   c1 receives the OLD c3 (11:00), then c3 becomes 11:01.
